---
/**
 * English-only guide for the compass silo.
 * URL: /onlinecompass/blog/online-compass-without-an-app/
 *
 * Intent: "online compass without app", "compass in browser", "phone compass
 * in chrome". Content static hai (i18n blog data par depend nahi karta) taaki
 * naya post publish karte waqt koi locale file chhedni na pade.
 */
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const title = 'Online Compass Without an App: Use Your Phone as a Compass';
const description =
  'Use your phone as a compass in the browser — no app, no install. What the magnetometer reads, how to calibrate in one tap, and when a browser compass is enough.';
const keywords =
  'online compass without app, compass in browser, phone compass chrome, browser compass no download, use phone as compass, online compass free';

const sections: { heading: string; paragraphs: string[]; list?: string[]; table?: { headers: string[]; rows: string[][] } }[] = [
  {
    heading: 'Why a browser compass beats another app install',
    paragraphs: [
      'If you need a direction once — which way is north, where does the wall face, which side of the building gets the morning sun — installing a 40 MB app for that is a bad trade. A browser compass opens in a second, reads the same magnetometer the app would read, and leaves nothing behind on your phone.',
      'The catch is that a browser compass depends on two things apps hide from you: permission and calibration. Once you know how both work, the reading is as good as any free app, and often better because nothing is layered on top of the number.',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'What the browser can and cannot read',
    paragraphs: [
      'Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge and Samsung Internet all expose device orientation to a web page, which is how a page can turn your phone into a compass. The sensor involved is the magnetometer — a tiny chip that measures the earth’s magnetic field — helped by the accelerometer, which tells the page how the phone is tilted.',
      'What the browser cannot do is read that sensor before you give permission, and on iOS it also needs a motion-and-orientation prompt that only appears after a tap. That is not a bug in the website; it is the platform protecting a sensor that can fingerprint a device. So every honest browser compass starts with a button, not a needle.',
    ],
    list: [
      'Android (Chrome, Samsung Internet, Firefox): permission shows as a single “Allow” banner.',
      'iPhone / iPad (Safari, Chrome, Firefox): a tap is required with iOS 13+, and the prompt appears once per site visit.',
      'Desktop or laptop: there is no magnetometer, so a compass cannot show a real heading — the page says so instead of inventing a number.',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Calibrating in one tap (and why figure-eights still matter)',
    paragraphs: [
      'A magnetometer is easily confused by nearby metal: a laptop lid, a phone case with magnets in it, a car dashboard, a steel desk. When the sensor is confused the needle drifts slowly even while you stand still — the classic “my compass is spinning” symptom.',
      'The fix is the same one map apps use. Hold the phone away from metal, move it in a slow figure-eight three or four times, then open the compass and tap calibrate. On the online compass the calibrate control is on the compass page itself, so you can re-zero anywhere without losing your place.',
      'If you are checking a wall or a gate, keep the phone flat rather than upright. Tilt changes the maths: upright, the phone reports the heading of its back face, which is noisier than a flat reading.',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Reading the dial correctly',
    paragraphs: [
      'A compass gives you a bearing between 0° and 359°, measured clockwise from north. North is 0°, east is 90°, south is 180°, west is 270°. The letters N, NE, E, SE, S, SW, W, NW are just shorthand for the same numbers.',
      'Two habits make readings usable. First, take the reading twice — once as you hold it, once after turning the phone 90° — and check the numbers agree. Second, remember that magnetic north is not true north: depending on where you are the difference is a few degrees, and for anything structural you should adjust for it.',
    ],
    table: {
      headers: ['Bearing', 'Direction', 'What people mean by it'],
      rows: [
        ['0° / 360°', 'North', 'Facing the reference direction of every map'],
        ['45°', 'North-east', 'Between north and east'],
        ['90°', 'East', 'Sunrise on an equinox, roughly'],
        ['180°', 'South', 'Opposite the reference direction'],
        ['270°', 'West', 'Sunset on an equinox, roughly'],
      ],
    },
  },
  {
    heading: 'When you still want a real compass or an app',
    paragraphs: [
      'A browser compass is a fast second opinion, not a surveying instrument. If you are navigating off-trail for hours, an app that caches maps and uses GPS heading is a better companion, and a baseplate compass with a mirror remains the tool that never runs out of battery.',
      'For everything else — which way the garden faces, where to point a satellite dish, whether a photo was shot facing the sea — the browser is enough, and it works on the phone you already have open.',
    ],
  },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does an online compass work without installing an app?',
    a: 'Yes. The browser reads the same magnetometer an app would read, using the device orientation API. You only need to allow motion and orientation access once per visit — no download, no account, nothing installed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does my browser compass spin or drift?',
    a: 'Usually nearby metal or magnets — a phone case, a laptop, a car dashboard — or a sensor that has not been calibrated since the device booted. Move away from metal, trace a figure-eight a few times and tap calibrate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work on iPhone?',
    a: 'Yes, in Safari and in Chrome on iOS. iOS asks for motion and orientation permission after a tap, so press the button on the compass page and allow access when the prompt appears.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does a laptop show no direction?',
    a: 'Laptops do not contain a magnetometer. A page that shows a heading on a desktop without any sensor is guessing; a correct page tells you the sensor is missing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the reading magnetic north or true north?',
    a: 'The sensor gives magnetic north. True north differs by a few degrees depending on your location (magnetic declination), so adjust the number if you are aligning something structural.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} keywords={keywords} type="article" chrome="instrument">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a
      href={translatePath('/onlinecompass/blog/')}
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Compass Blog</a
    >
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A browser compass is the fastest way to answer “which way is north?” without installing anything. This guide
      explains what the phone actually measures, how to calibrate it, and where the limits are. If you just want the
      reading, open the <a href={translatePath('/onlinecompass/')} class="text-link underline">online compass</a> and
      allow the sensor.
    </p>

    {sections.map((section) => (
      <div class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-4">{section.heading}</h2>
        {section.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
          <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">{paragraph}</p>
        ))}
        {section.list && (
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
            {section.list.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
        {section.table && (
          <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-canvas-elevated">
                  {section.table.headers.map((h) => (
                    <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">{h}</th>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {section.table.rows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    {row.map((cell) => (
                      <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">{cell}</td>
                    ))}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    ))}

    <div class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-6">FAQ</h2>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-0 border border-hairline rounded-md overflow-hidden">
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <details class="border-b border-hairline last:border-b-0">
            <summary class="font-sans text-base font-medium text-ink px-4 py-3 cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
              {item.q}
              <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-mute">▾</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed px-4 pb-3">{item.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mt-10 italic">
      Next: read the dial properly in <a href={translatePath('/onlinecompass/blog/how-to-read-a-compass-dial/')} class="text-link underline">how to read a compass dial</a>,
      or check tilt with the <a href={translatePath('/onlinebubblelevel/')} class="text-link underline">online bubble level</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>

<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />
